# arvo-bitmask. Design

Fixed-width bitmask types and bit-matrix adjacency structures.
Cross-cutting foundation for arvo-graph, arvo-sparse, arvo-comb,
and any consumer that needs set-level adjacency over bit storage.
`#![no_std]`, no alloc, const-generic sizing.

Currently, the crate depends on `arvo-storage` (for
`Bits<N, S, Sign>` and the strategy markers), `arvo-bits-contracts`
(for the trait surface: `HasBitWidth`, `BitAccess`, `BitSequence`,
`BitLogic`), and `arvo-mask-contracts` (this crate hosts the
concrete `MaskOps<W>` impls). Bitmasks are not numeric types. They
do bitwise operations (OR, AND, popcount), not arithmetic (Add,
Mul, Sqrt). The type families are different.

Currently, the per-width parallel concretes (Mask256,
BitMatrix64, BitMatrix256) collapsed onto generic `Mask<W>` and
`BitMatrix<W, const N: Cap>` chassis. The `WideBits`
BitPrim plumbing (#311) lets `Bits<256, Hot, Unsigned>` reach the
bit-level surface that `Mask<W>` requires; the same chassis handles
narrower widths (`Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>`) and wider widths up to
the arvo's 256-bit cap uniformly.

## NodeId

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
pub struct NodeId(pub USize);
```

Node index newtype. Wraps `USize` for type safety. Prevents mixing
node indices with bit positions, column indices, or other counts.
Used by `BitMatrix` and all arvo-graph algorithms. Zero cost after
compilation.

## Bitmask types

`Mask<W>` is the generic fixed-width bitmask chassis. `W` is a
bit-bearing word that satisfies `BitSequence + BitAccess + Copy +
Default`. The bitmask width follows from `<W as HasBitWidth>::WIDTH`.

`W` in regular use is a `Bits<N, S, Sign>` from arvo-storage. For
single-word storage, `Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>` (formerly the Mask64
payload) is the canonical W. For 256-wide storage backed by the
`WideBits<32, A16>` (32 bytes, 16-byte alignment for Hot at N > 128), `Bits<256, Hot, Unsigned>`
is the canonical W. Currently, closed the gap: the
arvo's `BitsContainerFor<N, Sign>` projection routes both cases
through the same trait-method composition, so `Mask<W>` no longer
needs a per-width parallel struct.

The chassis is type-generic, not const-generic. The `W` bound carries
the N / S / Sign axes through the Bits-shape; the trait constraints
on `W` express the chassis's needs without forcing every signature to
repeat them.

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Eq, PartialEq)]
pub struct Mask<W>
where
 W: BitSequence + BitAccess + Copy + Default,
{
 pub word: W,
}
```

Concrete shipping shapes:

- `Mask<Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>>`: backed by `u64` via the
 `BitsContainerFor<64, Unsigned>` projection. Single-word storage,
 single-instruction set ops on x86 (`or` / `and` / `xor` / `not`),
 identical instructions on aarch64.
- `Mask<Bits<256, Hot, Unsigned>>`: backed by
 `AlignedWideBits16<32>` via `BitsContainerFor<256, Unsigned>` (32
 bytes, 16-byte aligned for the Hot SIMD baseline; Warm / Cold /
 Precise project to `WideBits<32>` at 1-byte alignment). Multi-word
 storage with the unroll handled by the `WideBits` BitPrim impls.
 Loop-free, branch-free composition.

Narrower widths are equally valid: `Mask<Bits<32, Hot, Unsigned>>`
gives a 32-element bitmask backed by `u32`. The chassis works for
any W that the trait bounds accept.

`arvo-bitmask::Mask<W>` (the chassis struct) and
`arvo-mask-contracts::MaskOps` (the abstract operation contract)
are both shipped. The trait surface lets generic-over-mask consumer
code bind to the contract without naming the storage shape.
This dropped the prior `const W: Width` parameter on
`MaskOps`. Width information lives on the implementor's underlying
word type. The trait is now uniform across mask widths.
Implementations are documented under `Const-trait surface` below.

## Set operations

All operations delegate to arvo-bits trait methods on the backing
word type. `Mask<W>` routes through `BitLogic + BitAccess +
BitSequence` on `W` uniformly; the the WideBits impls
handle the multi-word unroll at the storage layer, so the chassis
methods stay one-line trait-method delegations.

```rust
fn union(self, other: Self) -> Self // BitLogic::bitor
fn intersection(self, other: Self) -> Self // BitLogic::bitand
fn difference(self, other: Self) -> Self // self & !other via BitLogic
fn complement(self) -> Self // BitLogic::bitnot
fn is_empty(self) -> Bool // BitSequence::is_zero
fn intersects(self, other: Self) -> Bool // bitand + is_zero negation
fn contains(self, pos: USize) -> Bool // BitAccess::bit
fn insert(&mut self, pos: USize) // BitAccess::with_bit_set
fn remove(&mut self, pos: USize) // BitAccess::with_bit_cleared
fn count(self) -> USize // BitSequence::count_ones
```

### Implementation

Single-instruction on the backing primitive: `bitor` / `bitand` /
`bitxor` / `bitnot` on `Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>` map to `or` / `and`
/ `xor` / `not` on x86 (identical on aarch64). On
`Bits<256, Hot, Unsigned>` the WideBits BitPrim
impls issue four 64-bit-equivalent ops loop-free. Bit scanning uses
`BitSequence::trailing_zeros` / `leading_zeros` directly; iterator
state carries `Bits<N, S, Sign>` (not raw container primitives).

Two sites retain compiler-mandated primitive types, annotated with
`lint:allow(...) reason:...; tracked: #N`:

- `matrix.rs`: `cap_size(c: Cap) -> usize`. Rust arrays `[T; N]`
 require `N: usize` at the language grammar level. Tracked #121.
- `node.rs`: `impl PartialOrd for NodeId`. Trait method
 `partial_cmp` returns `Option<Ordering>` by core contract.
 Tracked #115.
- `ops.rs`: `impl Iterator` blocks for `SetBitsIter<W>`. Trait
 method `next` returns `Option<Self::Item>` by core contract.
 Tracked #115.

No other raw primitives live in arvo-bitmask sources.

### Const-trait surface

`Mask<W>` ships const-routed inherent constants and `impl const`
on the bit-op family:

```rust
impl<W> Mask<W>
where
 W: BitSequence + BitAccess + Copy + Default + [const] Identity,
{
 pub const fn empty() -> Self { <Self as Identity>::ZERO }
}

impl<W> Mask<W>
where
 W: BitSequence + BitAccess + Copy + Default + [const] Bounded,
{
 pub const fn full() -> Self { <Self as Bounded>::MAX }
}
```

`impl const BitAnd`, `impl const BitOr`, `impl const BitXor`,
`impl const Not` on `Mask<W>`. The `feature(const_ops)` flag enables
these.

This routed `Mask<W>::empty()` and
`Mask<W>::full()` through `<Self as Identity>::ZERO` and
`<Self as Bounded>::MAX` per audit Finding 11. The hand-coded
defaults via `W::default()` retire; the const-trait route is
canonical.

`arvo-mask-contracts::MaskOps` ships as a
single blanket impl over the generic chassis:

```rust
impl<W> const MaskOps for Mask<W>
where
 W: Copy + Default
 + [const] BitAccess
 + [const] BitLogic
 + [const] BitSequence
 + [const] Bounded
 + [const] Identity,
{
 // Bodies delegate to the inherent surface on Mask<W>.
}
```

One blanket impl covers every concrete `Mask<W>` instantiation. No
per-W concrete impls (`Mask<Bits<64,...>>`, `Mask<Bits<256,...>>`,
etc.) ship with this round. Per `arvo-always-optimal-internals.md`'s
naive-baseline-first principle, the blanket is the arvo's
correct-by-construction baseline; concrete W specialisations land
later only when bench evidence shows a win (e.g., a SIMD shortcut
that the generic body cannot express). `min_specialization` stays
unused this round and enables when a future concrete override is
introduced. See `BACKLOG.md` for the gating.

Truncation under `Narrow<T>` composes through
`BitPrim::mask_low(n)` from arvo-bits-contracts at the bare-primitive
layer. Currently, dropped the prior
`MaskOps::mask_for_width(n: USize) -> Self` method (zero callers at
the trait surface; consumers already routed through `BitPrim`).

`BitMatrix<W, const N: Cap>` similarly carries const constructors
and const inherent methods where the underlying op chain supports
const evaluation.

## Bit scanning

Iterate over set bits efficiently, built on arvo-bits contracts:

```rust
fn lowest_set(self) -> USize // BitSequence::trailing_zeros
fn highest_set(self) -> USize // BitSequence::leading_zeros then subtract
fn iter_set_bits(self) -> impl Iterator<Item = USize>
```

`iter_set_bits` implemented via `trailing_zeros` + clear-lowest-bit
loop. Zero allocation, compiles to `rbit`+`clz` on aarch64, `tzcnt`
on x86.

## Bit-matrix

`BitMatrix<W, const N: Cap>` is the generic adjacency-matrix chassis.
Up to N nodes with N's bit width matching W's logical width, stored
as `[Mask<W>; cap_size(N)]`. Row `i` has bit `j` set if edge `i ->
j` exists. `Cap` is arvo's const-generic capacity newtype;
`cap_size(c: Cap) -> usize` is a local `const fn` that unwraps the
newtype for array sizing (inline `.0.0` is rejected by nightly
`generic_const_exprs`). Return type must be raw `usize` by language
grammar constraint; tracked #121 for revisit when rustc's
const-eval over newtypes stabilises.

```rust
pub struct BitMatrix<W, const N: Cap>
where
 W: BitSequence + BitAccess + Copy + Default,
 [(); cap_size(N)]:,
{
 pub rows: [Mask<W>; cap_size(N)],
}

fn edge(i: NodeId, j: NodeId) -> Bool // BitAccess::bit on row i
fn set_edge(i: NodeId, j: NodeId) // BitAccess::with_bit_set
fn successors(i: NodeId) -> Mask<W> // row i (all outgoing edges)
fn predecessors(j: NodeId) -> Mask<W> // scan column j across all rows
fn transitive_closure(&mut self) // Warshall on bitmasks
```

This collapsed the prior parallel `BitMatrix64<N>` and
`BitMatrix256<N>` structs into the generic chassis. The arvo's
The `WideBits` BitPrim work made the previous "Two concrete types
... abstracting via a trait fights `generic_const_exprs`" trade-off
obsolete; the trait machinery now resolves cleanly across both
single-word and multi-word backings.

## Dirty propagation

```rust
fn propagate_dirty<W, const N: Cap>(
 dag: &BitMatrix<W, N>,
 dirty: &mut Mask<W>,
)
where
 W: BitSequence + BitAccess + Copy + Default,
 [(); cap_size(N)]:,
```

OR sequence through DAG adjacency. For each set bit in `dirty`, OR
in all its successors. Propagates "changed" status through the
dependency graph. One AND per node. For 50 nodes on
`Mask<Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>>`: 50 AND operations, around 50ns.

Generic primitive. Any consumer that needs to propagate a change
flag through DAG adjacency uses the same function across width
choices. Currently, collapsed the parallel `propagate_dirty_64`
and `propagate_dirty_256` shipping shapes into this single
chassis.

## Consumers

- **arvo-graph**: bit-matrix DAG representation for topo / rank / waist
- **arvo-sparse**: sparse matrix bitmask operations (RCM, D-M, block)
- **arvo-comb**: constraint bitmasks for feasibility predicates

Downstream consumers (pipeline engines, compilers) use these through
the algorithm crates above, or directly for access-set / dirty-bit
tracking.


Round-by-round history is recorded in `mock/research/arvo-bitmask-history.md` (Tier 2).
